sec.p:
The model was fitted iteratively. In the first step, we fitted the temporal kernel using a General Linear Model, i.e. a linear regression model of the form
y=Xβ+ϵ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle y=X \beta+\epsilon$$\end{document}
where y is the fluorescence trace of one ROI, X is the design matrix, β contains the fitted temporal kernel, and ε is the residual error. X has size T × K, where T is the number of samples in each calcium trace and K is the number of samples in each kernel. Xi,j equals the current response amplitude ar (initially set to 1) for trial r if trial r started i − j samples before time point i, and otherwise Xi,j = 0. The resulting kernel β was normalized to have an absolute maximum of 1.

In the second step, we fitted the response amplitudes in a second General Linear Model
y=Uα+ϵ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle y=U \alpha+\epsilon$$\end{document}
where y is again the fluorescence trace of one ROI, U is the design matrix for fitting trial amplitudes, α contains the fitted trial amplitudes, and ε is the residual error. If T is the number of samples in the calcium trace and R is the number of grating presentations, then U has size T × R, and α has size R × 1. Each column of U corresponds to one trial. Specifically, if trial r started at sample s, then
Ui,r={βi−s,0≤i−s<K0,otherwise \begin{document}$$\displaystyle U_{i, r}=\left\{\begin{array}{cl}\beta_{i-s}, & 0 \leq i-s<K \\ 0, & \text { otherwise }\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
where β is the fitted temporal kernel of length K.

We only considered units with a genuine RF (see RF mapping) and a recording had to have at least 10 such units. Units with outlier RF locations (elevation or azimuth was >5 median absolute deviations away from the median across the population) were excluded. We then fitted a linear mapping from brain coordinates (x, y) to RF azimuth az and elevation el:
az=a1+b1x+c1yel=a2+b2x+c2y \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & a z=a_{1}+b_{1} x+c_{1} y \\ & e l=a_{2}+b_{2} x+c_{2} y\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
To enforce orthogonality between the azimuth and elevation axes in brain space, we constrained the elevation coefficients (b2, c2) to be orthogonal to the azimuth coefficients (b1, c1):
b2=1/1+(b1/c1)2c2=(−b1/c1)/1+(b1/c1)2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & b_{2}=1 / \sqrt{1+\left(b_{1} / c_{1}\right)^{2}} \\ & c_{2}=\left(-b_{1} / c_{1}\right) / \sqrt{1+\left(b_{1} / c_{1}\right)^{2}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
Model parameters were obtained by weighted least squares, weighting each unit by the variance explained by its final spatio-temporal RF model, so that units with more reliable RF estimates contributed more strongly to the fit. The resulting transform defined the optimal scaling, translation, and rotation that minimized the distance between mapped RF locations and the fitted retinotopic coordinates for each recording.

list-item.p:
Each axis with azimuth Taz and elevation Tel was converted to Cartesian coordinates:


T=(cos⁡(Tel)cos⁡(Taz),cos⁡(Tel)sin⁡(Taz),sin⁡(Tel)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T=\left(\cos \left(T_{e l}\right) \cos \left(T_{a z}\right), \cos \left(T_{e l}\right) \sin \left(T_{a z}\right), \sin \left(T_{e l}\right)\right.$$\end{document}

A rotation by angle α around the +y axis (right-hand rule) yielded:


U=(Txcos⁡(α)+Tzsin⁡(α),Ty,−Txsin⁡(α)+Tzcos⁡(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle U=\left(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}} \cos (\alpha)+\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{z}} \sin (\alpha), T_{y},-\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}} \sin (\alpha)+\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{z}} \cos (\alpha)\right.$$\end{document}

The result was then transformed back to azimuth-elevation coordinates:


Taz=tan−1⁡(Uy,Ux),Tel=sin−1⁡(Uz)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T_{a z}=\tan ^{-1}\left(U_{y}, U_{x}\right), \quad T_{e l}=\sin ^{-1}\left(U_{z}\right)$$\end{document}

We converted the rotated axis (Taz, Tel) and the RF position (raz, rel) to Cartesian vectors:


T=(cos⁡(Tel)cos⁡(Taz),cos⁡(Tel)sin⁡(Taz),sin⁡(Tel)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T=\left(\cos \left(T_{e l}\right) \cos \left(T_{a z}\right), \cos \left(T_{e l}\right) \sin \left(T_{a z}\right), \sin \left(T_{e l}\right)\right.$$\end{document}



and


r=(cos⁡(rel)cos⁡(raz),cos⁡(rel)sin⁡(raz),sin⁡(rel)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle r=\left(\cos \left(r_{e l}\right) \cos \left(r_{a z}\right), \cos \left(r_{e l}\right) \sin \left(r_{a z}\right), \sin \left(r_{e l}\right)\right.$$\end{document}

For a longitudinal field, the local direction vector v at r was the component of T tangential to the sphere:


v=T−(T⊤r)r
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle v=T-\left(T^{\top} r\right) r$$\end{document}

For a latitudinal field, v was given by the cross-product:


v=r×T
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle v=r \times T$$\end{document}

We then projected v onto the tangent plane at r using basis vectors aligned with increasing azimuth and elevation:


eϕ=(−sin⁡(raz),cos⁡(raz),0)eθ=(−sin⁡(rel)cos⁡(raz),−sin⁡(rel)sin⁡(raz),cos⁡(rel)w=(v⊤eϕ,v⊤eθ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & e_{\phi}=\left(-\sin \left(r_{a z}\right), \cos \left(r_{a z}\right), 0\right) \\ & e_{\theta}=\left(-\sin \left(r_{e l}\right) \cos \left(r_{a z}\right),-\sin \left(r_{e l}\right) \sin \left(r_{a z}\right), \cos \left(r_{e l}\right)\right. \\ & w=\left(v^{\top} e_{\phi}, v^{\top} e_{\theta}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We computed the local direction angle x on the sphere:


x=tan−1⁡(w2/w1)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle x=\tan ^{-1}\left(w_{2} / w_{1}\right)$$\end{document}

Finally, we converted x to our convention (0° = rightward, 90° = downward) as:


z=2π−x
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle z=2 \pi-x$$\end{document}
